/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   FXN — the currency card's CURATED half. Hand-written, dated, sourced.

   It exists because of a hard limit rather than a preference: the relay banks
   NO FX PAIRS. DXY is the only currency instrument in the archive, so USD/JPY,
   the 30 July joint intervention, the BoJ path and the carry trade cannot be
   measured on this page — not measured badly, not measurable. They are also
   the part of the subject a reader most wants, so they are carried here as
   prose with dates, levels and sources attached, in exactly the relationship
   CATACT has to CATR: two authors in one card, labelled as two, on two
   different surfaces so nobody has to remember which is which.

   THREE RULES THIS BLOCK LIVES BY.

   1. `asof` is printed on the card, always. A curated block with no date is a
      block that becomes wrong silently. This one goes stale the moment the
      Bank of Japan meets, and the card should say how old it is rather than
      wait to be caught.

   2. Nothing in here is presented as measured. The intervention SIZE is a
      press report; the attribution of the 30 July move to it is CONSISTENT
      WITH the tape and is not proven by it, and `note` says so on the card.
      Two of the sources disagree about when the last joint action was — 2011
      or 1998 — and that disagreement is published rather than resolved by
      picking the one that reads better.

   3. THE CAVEAT IS LOAD-BEARING, not a disclaimer. The mechanism this half
      describes — a BoJ hike unwinding the yen carry trade and forcing
      deleveraging into US equities — is a TAIL event. It cannot show up in a
      six-minute correlation over six days no matter how carefully that
      correlation is computed. So the measured half of this card reading weak
      is NOT evidence against this half, and without that sentence the two
      halves of the card quietly contradict each other and the reader has to
      decide which one to believe.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
var FXN = {
  asof: "2026-08-05",
  ttl: "The yen, and why none of it is in the numbers above",
  /* The finding used to be the LAST sentence of a ninety-word paragraph, which
     is where a reader on a phone finds it after scrolling past the provenance.
     It leads now, and it is still self-contained — it names the date, the
     parties and the action, so nothing above it is needed to parse it. What
     moved out is provenance, and provenance is not a finding: it went into the
     first timeline row, where it is one tap away and costs six words of height
     instead of two hundred pixels. Nothing was deleted. */
  lead: "<b>The 30 July intervention worked on the yen and did nothing to the " +
        "dollar.</b> The yen leg is 13.6% of DXY; across the two sessions after " +
        "the United States joined Japan in supporting the yen, the yen gained " +
        "<b>3.8%</b> and DXY finished net <b>+0.07%</b>.",
  tl: [
    { w: "Background", t: "What was announced, and by whom", k: "was",
      x: "The first joint action since <b>2011</b> on one account and since " +
         "<b>1998</b> on another, and the sources genuinely disagree — the " +
         "disagreement is published here rather than resolved by picking whichever " +
         "reads better. It was confirmed publicly on Sunday <b>3 August</b>. The " +
         "dollar index fell <b>1.0%</b> on the week to a seven-week low, which is " +
         "the figure most reports led with and is not the one that matters here: " +
         "a week contains a great deal more than one afternoon." },
    /* k is what KIND of row this is, and it exists because the left column was
       always doing two jobs without saying so: three of these six are dates and
       three are not. "was" is settled history, "is" is the state of play, and
       "next" is the single row here that can still change — it names a release
       that has not happened, and on the day this block is read that release is
       tomorrow. It gets the accent. */
    { w: "Thu 30 Jul", t: "The intervention", k: "was",
      x: "Joint US–Japan action to support the yen. Tokyo may have sold around " +
         "<b>$58.97bn</b> in New York hours; the US Treasury commitment is " +
         "reported at <b>$5–10bn</b>. USD/JPY had made a pre-intervention high " +
         "of <b>163.99</b>." },
    { w: "Fri 31 Jul", t: "The Bank of Japan held at 1%", k: "was",
      x: "Held, but with what reporting called its <b>most explicit signal to " +
         "date</b> of an early hike. Odds on a September move went to <b>44%</b> " +
         "from around 30%." },
    { w: "Sun 3 Aug", t: "Confirmed", k: "was",
      x: "Both sides confirmed the action publicly. Bessent cited the " +
         "&ldquo;substantial undervaluation of the yen&rdquo;; Trump framed it " +
         "as friendship. DXY printed a seven-week low of <b>99.42</b>." },
    /* Every number in this entry and the next is a USD/JPY level, which on a card
       about the DOLLAR is the most invertible quantity anywhere on the page: the
       pair going UP is the dollar up, the yen weaker, and the intervention being
       given back. The entry used to open "USD/JPY 157.47 post-intervention, having
       surged as much as 1.4% to 155.20" — whose grammatical subject is the pair,
       and a pair cannot surge from 157.47 to 155.20. The subject meant was the
       YEN. The lead paragraph four hundred pixels above already has it right
       ("the yen gained 3.8% while DXY finished net +0.07%"), so the card was
       contradicting itself about which side of the pair it was describing, in the
       one block where there is no arrow, no colour and no bar to check it
       against. Same species as the catalyst board's "Upside tail": a label whose
       plain reading is the opposite of its meaning. The convention is now stated
       once, in the first clause, and every level below inherits it. */
    { w: "Levels now", t: "Where it left the pair", k: "is",
      x: "The pair is <b>USD/JPY</b> throughout, so <b>higher is a weaker yen</b> " +
         "and the intervention being given back. It sits at <b>157.47</b>, down " +
         "from a pre-intervention <b>163.99</b>: the yen up <b>3.8%</b> across two " +
         "sessions, and up as much as a further 1.4% at the pair&rsquo;s low of " +
         "<b>155.20</b>. Floor now read at <b>155</b>, resistance <b>160</b>, " +
         "Fibonacci target <b>162–163</b>. Japanese corporate hedgers are " +
         "benchmarked at <b>150</b>." },
    { w: "Ahead", t: "What re-tests it", k: "next",
      x: "<b>US payrolls on 7 August</b> is named as the trigger that could " +
         "re-test <b>160</b> &mdash; which on the convention above is most of the " +
         "intervention handed back. The remaining BoJ meetings this year are " +
         "<b>16–17 Sep</b>, <b>29–30 Oct</b> and <b>17–18 Dec</b>. Japan&rsquo;s " +
         "prior solo effort ran <b>¥11.7T ($72.52bn)</b> across April–May 2026." },
    { w: "Elsewhere", t: "The other legs of the index", k: "is",
      x: "EUR is <b>57.6%</b> of DXY against the yen&rsquo;s 13.6%, and EUR/USD " +
         "stabilised near <b>1.15</b> with speculative net EUR shorts at " +
         "<b>$10.3bn</b> as of 28 July — the largest since February 2020. " +
         "GBP is 11.9% and CAD 9.1%. Those are the legs that absorbed the yen&rsquo;s " +
         "move and left the index flat." },
  ],
  note: "The tape on 30 July is CONSISTENT WITH the reported intervention and " +
        "does not prove it: the largest half-hour in the whole archive is that " +
        "afternoon's US cash open, and its shape — dollar down hard, gold up " +
        "harder, futures up — is what a dollar-selling operation looks like. It " +
        "is also what a dovish repricing looks like. This page cannot tell them " +
        "apart and does not claim to. Note too that a −0.62% move in DXY is " +
        "about three times what the yen leg alone can arithmetically produce.",
  caveat: "None of the above can be measured on this page and none of it should " +
          "be read as contradicted by the correlations above. The mechanism that " +
          "matters — a BoJ hike unwinding the yen carry trade and forcing " +
          "deleveraging into US equities — is a <b>tail event</b>. A six-minute " +
          "correlation over six days cannot see one, however carefully it is " +
          "computed. A weak measured tie and a live narrative risk are not in " +
          "conflict; they are answers to two different questions.",
  src: [
    { t: "Al Jazeera — Japan and US confirm rare joint intervention",
      u: "https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/8/3/japan-and-us-confirm-rare-joint-intervention-to-prop-up-yen" },
    { t: "IG — Yen intervention outlook: USD/JPY, dollar index and EUR/USD",
      u: "https://www.ig.com/en/news-and-trade-ideas/yen-intervention-implications-for-dxy-eur-260805" },
    { t: "Investing.com — USD/JPY intervention risks: key levels and scenarios",
      u: "https://www.investing.com/news/stock-market-news/usdjpy-intervention-risks-key-levels-and-scenarios-after-historic-yen-defense-93CH-4836371" },
    { t: "Fortune — Yen rises amid speculation of more intervention",
      u: "https://fortune.com/2026/08/03/yen-rises-amid-speculation-of-more-intervention-after-u-s-support/" },
    { t: "Japan Times — Yen strengthens on possible intervention",
      u: "https://www.japantimes.co.jp/business/2026/07/31/markets/yen-intervention-breaking-2026/" },
    { t: "MNI — Bank of Japan meeting calendar",
      u: "https://www.mnimarkets.com/calendars/bank-of-japan-meeting-calendar" },
