  ES: {
    label:"ES", unit:"pt", dp:1, seed:7717.0,
    sub:"Quoted in FUTURES rather than cash S&P, so this ladder and the liquidity map below share one frame. Prefer ES ≈ SPX × 1.0037 and leave it alone: the board carries 1.0037 against a basis re-measured at Tuesday’s 20:00:00Z cash close of 1.003639, an error of 6.1e-5, which is 0.47 of a point on SPX at 7,735.60 and well inside a single 2.5-point ES bin. ||| Three modes and an empty middle · why the basis held · the invalid cross-check ||| Basis RE-MEASURED at Tuesday’s 20:00:00Z cash close on the OPEN of the 20:00 bar, because that bar’s close is already the 20:05 print: ES 7,763.75 futures against cash SPX 7,735.60, a ratio of 1.003639. This is the measurement that was mandated for Wednesday and it came back clean. The question was whether 1.00365 would hold or revert toward the 1.0040 this board used to publish. It held: 1.004043 on Friday, 1.003651 on Monday, 1.003639 on Tuesday — the step down happened once, over the weekend, and then the level stayed put.|The second estimator, built from what this board called same-instant medians, read 1.00397, 1.00338, 1.00349 and was published here as an unexplained disagreement. It is now explained, and the verdict is harder than noisy: it is INVALID AS CONSTRUCTED. Each row of the quote archive carries one timestamp and thirteen prices, but every price arrives at its own provider delay — ES is served 602 seconds old and cash SPX 61 — so every ratio the estimator formed divided a futures price from ten minutes ago into a cash price from one. The legs were never same-instant. Re-measuring with the futures leg shifted back to match walks it toward the settle-anchored figure on all three sessions, and further with each row of shift: 4 August reads 1.003237 unshifted, 1.003524 at one row and 1.003744 at two, against a bar-open 1.003639. It does not land exactly because the true offset is about one and a half rows and the archive steps only in whole ones, so a discrete shift can bracket the answer and cannot hit it. Nothing on this board moves: the settle-anchored bar-open method was always the source and it stays the source. What would promote the cross-check to a second source is a finer archive cadence or a feed that stamps each leg — not a re-run of the same arithmetic.|Every level below is rebuilt from the same 5,510-bar, 679.6-hour, 2.5-point-bin profile and the same session-weighted stop model the liquidity map publishes. What the rebuild found: the 70% value area is 7,682.5 to 7,825.0 and the market is trading clear above it, with 99.20% of the window at or below 7,825.0 There is a sixty-five-bin shelf from 7,665.0 to 7,825.0 holding 78.43% of everything traded, a five-bin void beneath it holding 0.80%, and then the old distribution. Three modes, not one, and the middle one is empty.|On the reach model this contract still under-predicts the bell — cash-hour error −22% on 126 windows against −25% last time — so every excursion figure here understates what 13:30Z is capable of. European −15%, Asian −4%, and Asian is as calibrated as this measurement gets. Both boards kept the sign of their grades this time, after swapping them inside five days at the last rebuild. Two sessions is not stability, but it is the first evidence in either direction. The price box takes the tape rather than a hand-typed settlement.",
