/* LADDERS-END */

var SCENARIOS = [
  { id:"A", name:"The break was a flush and the shelf is reclaimed", prob:45, st:"good",
    tag:"The condition this branch wrote for itself has FIRED: ES closed the cash session inside its shelf &mdash; the 19:55Z auction bar, seventy-eight thousand contracts, closed 7,778.50 &mdash; the full cash-session close this branch said would make it live again. RTY came within two points of joining it and did not. ||| Each contract still has a dense shelf, a hollow directly beneath it, and the balance this board was describing two days ago beneath the hollow; that part is measured rather than argued. ES reclaimed its shelf three times today &mdash; the payrolls half-hour, then again after a midday fade pressed the floor from beneath, and then for keeps: the cash session CLOSED at 7,778.50, inside 7,760.00 to 7,785.00, after trading 7,786.75 through the shelf&rsquo;s top. It is {{px ES}} in the evening stub. RTY got to 3,041.00 on Thursday, 3,040.00 at the print, and 3,046.80 in the afternoon &mdash; its first trade INSIDE the lost shelf &mdash; but closed 3,040.90, two points under the 3,043.00 floor: its half of the reclaim printed intraday and did not close. A reclaim that printed and failed on both contracts was why this branch was cut to 30; the payrolls bid took it back to 40; the cash close its own text demanded is why it carries 45 tonight &mdash; with the two points RTY still owes named as the reason it is not higher. What still argues for it is the one thing that has not stopped: the shelf gets HEAVIER while it breaks. {{above RTY 3043.00}}% of the window traded at or above 3,043.00, against the 11.06 per cent the same measurement carried yesterday, because the selling kept leaving volume behind in it on the way down. Inventory changing hands is not inventory leaving &mdash; and today the hands that took it showed up: the afternoon traded INTO the shelf on both contracts, and on ES it closed there.",
    rows:[
      ["RTY", "The shelf runs <b>3,043.00</b> to <b>3,050.00</b>, eight bins, and <b>3,046.00</b> is the heaviest of them at {{share RTY 3046.00}}%, though no longer the cash-session point of control &mdash; the payrolls volume moved that crown down to the <b>3,035.00</b> bin. Spot at {{px RTY}} finished the cash session two points of travel from the floor it lost &mdash; it was thirty-four points away at the European low, thirteen after the payrolls half-hour, and 3,046.80 printed INSIDE the shelf in the afternoon before the close backed off to just beneath it. The structure had never had to defend itself, then it did and it lost, and the walk back this row was waiting for has now been attempted and failed &mdash; RTY traded 3,041.00 at 22:20 HKT, two points short of 3,043.00, and gave the whole move back inside two hours. A failed reclaim is worse for this branch than no reclaim at all, because it prices who was willing to buy it. That was Thursday&rsquo;s verdict; the payrolls print has forced a second vote &mdash; a 95th-percentile half-hour carried RTY from the day&rsquo;s low region to 3,040.00, one point shy of Thursday&rsquo;s failed-reclaim high, and it now trades from the middle of the ladder rather than staring up at it from the floor. The afternoon held the third vote: 3,046.80 traded, THROUGH the floor and inside the shelf, before the close settled two points back beneath it &mdash; each attempt has ended higher than the last, which is the shape of a level being worn down rather than defended. The ladder this branch had to climb was climbed on Friday and eaten on the way up, and the weekend has put one rung back &mdash; on paper rather than on the tape, which is a distinction worth the sentence. WITHDRAWN &mdash; this row said <b>3,030.44</b> was still absent and that nothing had re-formed there. Both halves were false the day they were written, and what has happened since is the more useful fact: the pool that re-formed keys <b>{{pool RTY 3029.48}}</b>, carries {{turns RTY 3029.48}} turns and was last touched {{age RTY 3029.48}} hours ago, and it has drifted to the far edge of the tolerance that lets this page call two prices one object. A little further and they stop being one object &mdash; not because anything traded there, but because a cluster boundary moved under a level nobody has been near in days. Emptied, rebuilt and then quietly re-keyed is a different history from emptied and left empty, and the last of those three steps is the one no chart will show you. The rung above it came back on the map overnight, and this line then said it had gone again. That claim is WITHDRAWN rather than restated: what stands in that band now is {{pool RTY 3038.54}}, and an absence cannot be asserted at a price a pool occupies. The level was listed, de-listed and listed again inside three sessions before this row stopped watching it, which is the behaviour worth carrying forward &mdash; not any one of those states. Twice in three sessions this level has been listed and de-listed without the market doing anything there, which is worth more as a warning about the measurement than as news about the level &mdash; a cluster boundary a few tenths wide will hand you a pool and take it away again on the same tape. Overhead is NOT unchanged, and that is the finding. The rung that stood at the top of the shelf is no longer on the map at all &mdash; it was not taken, it dissolved as Monday and Tuesday re-formed the clustering beneath it. <b>{{gone RTY 3046.45}}</b> is WITHDRAWN &mdash; nothing is clustered there now &mdash; and so is {{gone RTY 3036.52}}. THE CONSOLIDATION IS THE FINDING, and it is larger than either withdrawal. Three shelves this row described separately inside a ten-point band have become one object over eleven days: <b>{{pool RTY 3038.54}}</b> now holds {{turns RTY 3038.54}} turns, {{mix RTY 3038.54 RTH}} of them cash-made, last touched {{age RTY 3038.54}} hours ago. It is not one of the three relocated. At 0.23, 2.02 and 7.91 bins from the prices they were keyed to, no single predecessor is near enough to be the same structure under a new key; what happened is that a band this row read as three separate rungs traded into one shelf, and the count that matters is the new object&rsquo;s, not the sum of theirs. <b>3,002.00</b> is the other anchor and it is still the low of the trailing day, thirty-nine points beneath the close &mdash; the standing start at the bottom of the day was converted into the top of the ladder inside one session. Note what kept moving, because it finally moved the other way: 3,015.00 was the low when this row was first written and price made a lower one three times; today it made a HIGHER high three times through the payrolls session and the afternoon."],
      ["ES",  "The same structure at a different scale, and the day ended it split finer than any session yet: ES closed the cash session inside its shelf; RTY traded inside its own &mdash; 3,046.80 against a 3,043.00 floor &mdash; and could not close there. The convergence of damage this row opened with is gone; what is left is a two-point question on the smaller contract. <b>7,760.00</b> to <b>7,785.00</b> is eleven bins, {{range ES 7760.00 7785.00}}% of the window between them, and still the densest stretch on the ES map. What it carries has now resolved three times in one day. At the print the payrolls bid consumed everything resting at <b>7,766.49</b>; the level rebuilt within the hour as price camped on it; the afternoon walk consumed it AGAIN on the way to the close &mdash; and it has REBUILT A THIRD TIME since. <b>{{pool ES 7764.68}}</b> now carries {{turns ES 7764.68}} turns &mdash; heavier than either of the rebuilds described above, and the heaviest this level has held all week. That is the SIXTH state of one level: emptied, defended-and-losing, consumed, rebuilt, consumed again, and now rebuilt heavier than before. The absence this row used to record has been overtaken; the level is back and it is thicker than it was. This row spent the week reading the level as defended-and-losing while price walked away; the day validated the defence twice over, and the second consumption is the cleaner signal: resting interest at the centre of the shelf keeps getting filled in the buyers&rsquo; direction and keeps re-forming until it is filled again. It sits under the cash point of control, which Wednesday's session moved to <b>7,772.50</b>, so the price the session keeps turning at and the price it spent its time at are the same price. What has changed is the distance. The trailing range now runs <b>7,725.50</b> to <b>7,786.75</b>; ES spent the European morning near the bottom of it, the payrolls half-hour carried it back to the top quarter, and the cash close printed 7,778.50 &mdash; the top sixth of the day, inside the shelf. The void underneath did not fill on the way up: from <b>7,640.00</b> to <b>7,745.00</b> there are forty-three bins, {{range ES 7640.00 7745.00}}% of the window between all of them, {{rangebin ES 7640.00 7745.00}}% a bin against a 0.53% average for the contract &mdash; ES fell into that stretch on Thursday and has been lifted back out, so the hollow is once again beneath price rather than around it. <b>7,636.42</b> is the first real structure beneath the fresh pools, well over a hundred points down. The narrowing this row was watching resolved in one half-hour: the reclaim both contracts needed, ES was handed intraday &mdash; RTY&rsquo;s never quite printed &mdash; and what neither has is the cash close that turns an intraday visit into a reclaim."],
      ["HSI", "The reference close stays flagged and stays untrusted, so read the session in its own prices. Friday&rsquo;s HK session is the first counter-example in five days: it opened at 25,526.65, traded to 25,669.52, and finished at 25,668.03 &mdash; parked on its own high, the first session this week HK has closed on strength inside its own hours rather than selling it. One session against four is a change of behaviour rather than a change of regime. Note the direction, because it rhymes with the rest of this branch: HK stopped selling on the same day the payrolls bid stopped the RTY slide."]
    ],
    invalid:"CALL: this branch was tradeable on Thursday and it lost, and that is the first thing on it. The trigger as written &mdash; long ES on a five-minute close back above 7,760.00, stop under 7,746.75 &mdash; printed at 14:00Z on the leg that carried to 7,768.50, and the stop printed at 15:15Z on a five-minute close of 7,739.25. Call it twenty-two points against, taken exactly as specified, recorded here rather than quietly re-specified afterwards. The RTY half of the same call never triggered at all: it needed a cash bar closing back above 3,043.00 and the high of the day was 3,041.00, two points short &mdash; so the contract this board told you NOT to buy is the one that cost nothing. It is worth naming why the ES trigger was a bad trigger before it was a losing one. ES closed a five-minute bar on the other side of 7,760.00 twenty-five separate times that Thursday. A level-close entry on a price oscillating through that level is not an entry, it is a coin flip with commission attached, and the repair is a distance or a persistence filter rather than a different level: a close a full point clear of it, or two consecutive closes on the same side. RE-ENTRY, because this is a re-entry board and not a management one: the payrolls print has moved this branch from &lsquo;no long here&rsquo; to &lsquo;restore condition live&rsquo;. THE CONDITION HAS FIRED. This row demanded a full cash-session close inside 7,760.00 to 7,785.00 &mdash; not a five-minute close, not a payrolls spike &mdash; and the bell delivered exactly that: the closing auction printed 7,778.50, eighteen points inside the floor, after the fade this row feared pressed 7,760.00 from beneath at midday and was bought back. The slower timeframe was chosen for precisely today and it did its job in both directions: it kept this branch out of the morning whipsaw, and it certifies the reclaim now. What the weekend holds: the branch is live by its own written test; RTY&rsquo;s half remains unclosed (3,046.80 traded, 3,040.90 closed, floor at 3,043.00); and Monday opens on a gap this page cannot see past. WHAT WOULD BREAK IT from here: a Monday open that trades back under 7,760.00 and stays there through the first hour &mdash; a reclaim that fails over a weekend is Thursday&rsquo;s lesson at a larger scale. Two calibration notes that still apply. In cash hours the reach model UNDER-states ES travel by 25%, so ES levels arrive sooner than the arithmetic says once the bell rings; on RTY it OVER-states by 18% in cash hours, so RTY levels are further away than the board reads. And the archive behind this window is a SIX-MINUTE SAMPLE rather than a tape: it can establish that a level was crossed and can never establish that one was untouched." },
  { id:"B", name:"The break continues and the hollow does its work", prob:30, st:"warning",
    tag:"Both contracts confirmed it on Thursday; the payrolls print bought half of it back and the cash close bought most of the rest, which is why this branch ends the week second on the board. ||| RTY did not fall THROUGH the hollow on Thursday, it filled the hollow and then fell out of the bottom of it: between 3,019.00 and 3,042.00 there are twenty-four bins, and the 7.04% of the window they held when this branch was written has become {{range RTY 3019.00 3042.00}}% &mdash; {{rangebin RTY 3019.00 3042.00}}% a bin against a 0.65% average for the contract, so the stretch price was supposed to drop through ended up DENSER than the map around it. That read like a defeat for this branch and was scored the opposite while spot sat beneath the stretch; the payrolls half-hour has flipped the side that mattered. Spot is {{px RTY}}, back INSIDE the stretch, trading through inventory this branch needs to act as a ceiling. ES un-confirmed at the same moment and then buried the case at the bell: it CLOSED the cash session inside its shelf, at 7,778.50. This branch needed the print&rsquo;s move to fail &mdash; a 30-minute close back under 3,015.00 &mdash; and got the opposite: RTY closed twenty-six points above that line after trading inside its own shelf. What this branch keeps is the record, and one live fact: the RTY close still sits under 3,043.00, so the break has been un-broken everywhere except the one line that started it.",
    rows:[
      ["RTY", "Price WAS out of the hollow, out of the BOTTOM of it, for exactly one overnight session &mdash; the sentence this row waited three refreshes to write, un-written by the payrolls print inside half an hour. Between <b>3,019.00</b> and <b>3,042.00</b> there are twenty-four bins, and the 7.04% of the window they held when this row was written has become {{range RTY 3019.00 3042.00}}% &mdash; {{rangebin RTY 3019.00 3042.00}}% a bin against a 0.65% average for the contract, so the twenty-three points price was supposed to fall through ended up thicker than the map around them. The heaviest of them is <b>3,035.00</b> at {{share RTY 3035.00}}%, and every bit of that weight was laid down by the session that was meant to be falling through it. What matters is which side of it price stands on, and the payrolls half-hour changed the answer. Spot at {{px RTY}} is no longer beneath the stretch, it is back inside it, trading through the inventory this row read as a ceiling &mdash; and two full sessions have since been dealt inside it, which has rebuilt this part of the map rather than confirmed the old reading of it. Monday sold RTY 10.90 points while ES held its bid; Tuesday inverted that exactly, RTY taking 14.50 back while ES gave up 15.50 &mdash; opposite directions on consecutive days, both of them transacted here. What they left is three clusters where this row saw inventory it expected to be crossed once: <b>{{pool RTY 3029.48}}</b> and <b>{{pool RTY 3022.05}}</b>, plus a third that is now <b>{{gone RTY 3036.52}}</b> &mdash; WITHDRAWN, with nothing clustered at that price any more. The two that stand carry {{turns RTY 3029.48}} and {{turns RTY 3022.05}} turns respectively. That last one has been taken into {{pool RTY 3038.54}}, {{turns RTY 3038.54}} turns, which formed across the band above it. So this part of the map is no longer three objects rather than one: the upper two of the three have become the one. <b>3,030.44</b> was run clean out of the map by the payrolls bid, and it has since rebuilt &mdash; the first of the three clusters above IS that level re-formed, keyed <b>{{pool RTY 3029.48}}</b> and carrying {{turns RTY 3029.48}} turns. This row asserted it was gone for two days while listing it three sentences earlier. The rung above briefly re-appeared under a new key overnight and is now unlisted again, its turns having been Friday&rsquo;s all along &mdash; a boundary redrawn twice, not a level rebuilt. What this row was watching as ceiling was traded through on Friday, in the direction that argues against the branch writing it, and nothing since has tested that verdict. The day&rsquo;s high at <b>3,041.00</b> was the top of Thursday&rsquo;s failed attempt, and the payrolls push has now printed 3,040.00, one point shy of it &mdash; twice rejected two-and-change under the shelf floor is the strongest fact left on this row&rsquo;s side. <b>3,002.00</b> is the trailing day&rsquo;s low, thirty-nine points beneath the close: the fresh session opened in the bottom of the old one and the payrolls day lifted it all the way out. Underneath is where the map has gone thin, which is the other half of the change. The four bins from <b>3,015.00</b> to <b>3,018.00</b> are {{range RTY 3015.00 3018.00}}% of the window between all of them, {{rangebin RTY 3015.00 3018.00}}% a bin &mdash; thicker than the map&rsquo;s 0.65% average, and spot has been carried back above them &mdash; the thin stretch beneath is underfoot again rather than the air price was falling through. The thin part starts under 3,015.00 and runs down. Widen it and the picture holds: <b>2,988.00</b> to <b>3,015.00</b> is twenty-eight bins, {{range RTY 2988.00 3015.00}}% of the window between all of them, {{rangebin RTY 2988.00 3015.00}}% a bin. <b>3,005.00</b> at {{share RTY 3005.00}}% is the only thing resembling a shelf in the first twelve points down. Then the shelf Monday&rsquo;s cash session built, where it stops being thin: <b>2,988.00</b> is the densest bin in that stretch. The pool that anchored that stretch is no longer on the map &mdash; three sessions of trade above it re-formed the clustering, and the nearest thing beneath price now is <b>{{pool RTY 3004.01}}</b> at {{turns RTY 3004.01}} turns. The <b>2,951.11 pool</b> has sixteen turns, eight of them Asian, on a round number, and it is the oldest structure on this part of the map by a wide margin. Then <b>2,941.00</b>, and <b>2,936.00</b> &mdash; the old balance&rsquo;s heaviest bin, though no longer the point of control across all hours: one payrolls day moved that crown ninety-nine points up the map, to <b>3,035.00</b>."],
      ["ES",  "The trigger printed a third time and held into Thursday&rsquo;s close; the payrolls print has taken ES back above it, which un-does the difference this row was rewritten to record. The void under the shelf runs from <b>7,640.00</b> to <b>7,757.50</b>: forty-eight bins, {{range ES 7640.00 7757.50}}% of the window between all of them, up from 13.67% when this row was written. Most of that thickening is still at the ceiling &mdash; the five bins from <b>7,747.50</b> up come to {{range ES 7747.50 7757.50}}% between them, and <b>7,752.50</b> alone carries {{share ES 7752.50}}% &mdash; but spot has been carried back OVER that ceiling &mdash; the 13:00Z thirty-minute bar closed at 7,770.25, thirteen points above it. Take those five off and the body, <b>7,640.00</b> to <b>7,745.00</b>, forty-three bins, comes to {{range ES 7640.00 7745.00}}% of the window between all of them, {{rangebin ES 7640.00 7745.00}}% a bin against a 0.53% average for the contract. ES fell inside that on Thursday and has been lifted back out. A hundred points of thin, and spot spent the European morning in it &mdash; a sentence this row got to keep for one session before the print un-wrote it. What it is doing in there deserves more attention than the fact that it is there. <b>7,735.00</b> at {{share ES 7735.00}}% is the heaviest bin in the entire body of the void, with <b>7,732.50</b> at {{share ES 7732.50}}% just beneath it, and neither was there on Thursday morning, so Thursday&rsquo;s session filled the void from the top downward as it walked. That slow work was the shape of a void being worked rather than gapped &mdash; and it is why the payrolls reversal weighs so much here: levels built at walking pace are real, and they traded back through in one half-hour anyway. What it lands on is the densest thing ES has built all week. <b>7,632.50</b> at {{share ES 7632.50}}% is the heaviest bin down there, with <b>7,630.00</b> just under it at {{share ES 7630.00}}%, and the <b>7,636.42 pool</b> sits on top of them with twenty-four turns, twelve of them Asian and ten European, and it is the oldest structure in the cluster. That cluster is real support and it is well over a hundred points beneath the market. Beneath it <b>7,552.39</b> is a bin and NOT a pool &mdash; no resting turns are recorded there, only volume traded through it &mdash; and <b>7,470.00</b> is the floor of the balance the market left behind in July."],
      ["HSI", "HK&rsquo;s Friday session came and went without the US delivering anything to gap into &mdash; and then closed on its own high. Whatever this branch does next resolves in US hours and lands on Monday&rsquo;s HK open; carrying it over the weekend gap is a bet on Monday&rsquo;s US tape wearing an HSI ticker, the same trade with worse liquidity and a prior close this page has flagged."]
    ],
    invalid:"CALL: both triggers have now printed and the trade as written has worked, which is a thing this board should say as plainly when it happens as when it does not. On RTY the five-minute close under 3,043.00 came at Thursday&rsquo;s cash open. The sell zone this row specified &mdash; a failure in 3,038.00 to 3,043.00, stop above 3,043.00 &mdash; filled at 22:10 HKT when the reclaim ran to 3,041.00 and turned two points shy of the shelf&rsquo;s floor, and the stop was never threatened because 3,043.00 was never traded. First target 3,021.10 filled in the last half hour of the cash session. Second target 3,005.00 has FILLED &mdash; the low is 3,002.00, three points through it. On ES the restated trigger &mdash; a five-minute close below 7,746.75 &mdash; printed at 15:15Z at 7,739.25 and held through Thursday&rsquo;s close, giving none of it back inside that session. MANAGEMENT, for once, rather than re-entry: the first target is banked, so the runner is free. Stop to entry on the balance, and the level that matters for the rest of it is <b>3,015.00</b>, the floor of the four bins price has just dropped out of. While price stays under it the thin part of the map is doing the work; a reclaim of it puts price back inside a hollow that is now DENSER than the map around it, which is a different trade and not this one. UPDATE AT THE PRINT: that reclaim HAS fired &mdash; the 13:00Z thirty-minute bar closed at 3,032.40, seventeen points back inside the hollow &mdash; so by this row&rsquo;s own sentence the runner&rsquo;s edge is gone. The payrolls session printed 3,040.00 into the 3,038.00-to-3,043.00 entry zone, so the balance is in all likelihood flat at entry under the stop-to-entry rule &mdash; the exact fill decides that, not this page &mdash; and the afternoon settled direction by closing the cash session at 3,040.90, inside the zone itself. What the campaign banked before the print: first target in full, second target in full, and at worst a scratch on the runner. WHAT WOULD BREAK IT: RTY trading back up into 3,043.00 to 3,050.00 and leaving on the upside within the hour, which converts a shelf that broke into a shelf that was defended and is the strongest bullish signal available on this board. UPDATE AT THE CLOSE: the first half of that has happened &mdash; RTY traded 3,046.80, inside the zone &mdash; and the second half has not: it backed out to close 3,040.90, beneath the floor. Entered-and-rejected is not the signal; it is the zone doing its job as supply, and this branch survives the weekend on that two-point rejection. Short of that, the pace hazard this row named has resolved against the branch: the void ES was working one bin at a time gave back the whole walk in a single payrolls half-hour. The scheduled-headline warning written here belonged to an earlier session&rsquo;s clock and retires with the position; the rule it stated survives it &mdash; a thin book plus a scheduled print is how stops become suggestions &mdash; and the next such print belongs to the next brief&rsquo;s calendar, not to this card." },
  { id:"C", name:"It goes back up and retakes the highs", prob:25, st:"warning",
    tag:"A forecast on Tuesday, half-run on Wednesday, failed into Thursday &mdash; then handed a 95th-percentile payrolls print and a cash close that did most of the repair work, which is the whole of the news. ||| RTY has now traded <b>3,046.80</b>, inside the shelf it lost, eleven under the <b>3,058.00</b> top of its window &mdash; and closed two points beneath the shelf floor at <b>3,043.00</b>. ES traded <b>7,786.75</b>, through its shelf&rsquo;s top and thirty-three under its own <b>7,820.00</b>, and closed the cash session at 7,778.50. Neither contract has reached the highs this branch is named after, but both finished the week pointed at them rather than away from them, which no session since Tuesday could say. What kept this branch alive was what stood above and who built it, and half of that has been withdrawn by the tape rather than by me: the shelf-edge pool is off the map. What this row named above spot on RTY, <b>{{gone RTY 3046.45}}</b>, is WITHDRAWN &mdash; nothing is clustered there now, and the weight that was in that band sits in {{pool RTY 3038.54}} instead; the ES structure this row compared against, <b>{{gone ES 7820.25}}</b>, is WITHDRAWN &mdash; nothing is clustered there now &mdash; it has left the map, so there is no age left to quote and the comparison has lost one of its two sides. Where a week ago the only structure above was overnight inventory. A high the bell built is a high the bell has to defend or hand back.",
    rows:[
      ["RTY", "Above <b>3,049.00</b> the map still runs out, and today did not change that. {{above RTY 3049.00}}% of the window traded at or above 3,049.00 &mdash; {{abovebins RTY 3049.00}} bins out of {{bins RTY}}. Seven points higher it collapses: {{above RTY 3056.00}}% of the window traded at or above 3,056.00 &mdash; {{abovebins RTY 3056.00}} bins out of {{bins RTY}}. The high itself is <b>3,058.00</b>, one bin holding {{share RTY 3058.00}}% of the window, the top of the whole measurement and the thinnest print RTY has made in six days. That is a spike rather than an auction: price went up, found nobody, and left. What was up there is no longer up there. That pool has left the map without being traded through &mdash; RTY&rsquo;s high across Monday and Tuesday was 3,045.80, so price never reached it and it was not consumed; the cluster simply re-formed lower and the key went with it. The object this row called the one above spot, <b>{{gone RTY 3046.45}}</b>, is WITHDRAWN &mdash; nothing rests at that price now, so it has no turns, no age and no rank left to quote. What occupies the band is {{pool RTY 3038.54}} at {{turns RTY 3038.54}} turns, {{rank RTY 3038.54}}-heaviest of the RTY stop map by score. Everything scoring at or above it sits BELOW it in price, and Friday&rsquo;s climb took two of them: <b>3,030.44</b> was run straight through by the payrolls bid and has since been re-posted: it keys <b>{{pool RTY 3029.48}}</b> now, carrying {{turns RTY 3029.48}} turns. A level the map stops listing is showing you consumption, the one exit that kind of structure is built for, and Friday printed it twice on the way up &mdash; but consumption is not removal, and this one came back inside three sessions. One of the two flickered back onto the map under a shifted key overnight and has since dropped off it again, carrying turns that predated the reopen throughout; the honest reading is a clustering boundary moving twice, not stops being re-posted over a weekend. That is the finding &mdash; the ladder under the shelf was eaten by exactly the move this branch needed. It also sits on the upper edge of the <b>3,043.00</b> to <b>3,050.00</b> shelf RTY lost on Thursday, so this branch has to travel the whole shelf before it gets there: {{above RTY 3043.00}}% of the window traded at or above 3,043.00 &mdash; {{abovebins RTY 3043.00}} bins out of {{bins RTY}} &mdash; and price is underneath all of it. The measurement beneath has swung all the way to this branch&rsquo;s side in one day. WITHDRAWN &mdash; the all-hours value area no longer tops out at <b>3,060.00</b>, which it did when this was written, at the window high then &mdash; that ceiling stood at 3,020 this morning &mdash; and the busiest price across all hours is now <b>3,035.00</b>. The old balance at 2,936 no longer holds that crown; one payrolls day moved it ninety-nine points up the map. Value did not creep toward the highs, it relocated to them in a single session. Spot at {{px RTY}} finished the cash session inside the new value&rsquo;s upper half. What is left of the bear case here is one line: the shelf floor at 3,043.00, tagged to 3,046.80 and not closed above."],
      ["ES",  "The same region, a completely different answer, and this is the divergence that matters. {{above ES 7787.50}}% of the window traded at or above 7,787.50 &mdash; {{abovebins ES 7787.50}} bins out of {{bins ES}} &mdash; where RTY&rsquo;s equivalent stretch is nearly empty. {{above ES 7810.00}}% of the window traded at or above 7,810.00, {{abovebins ES 7810.00}} bins out of {{bins ES}}. Where RTY has a rounding error near its high, ES has an auction. The strongest single statement on this board about the ES upside is a boundary rather than a level. <b>7,820.00</b> is no longer the top of the window nor, measured across all hours, the upper edge of the 70% value area &mdash; so value expanded all the way to the high instead of being left behind by it. RTY could not say that this morning &mdash; its value area then topped out thirty-eight points beneath its window high &mdash; and by the close it nearly could: one payrolls session expanded RTY&rsquo;s value to within a point of its own high, so the divergence this row was built on has closed. What remains of the old balance is a bin rather than a pool, down there &mdash; the turns that made it are still in the profile, but no cluster keys on them any more. The pool this row quoted here yesterday is no longer on the map at all, which is its own small piece of evidence: the structure down there is thinning rather than building. WITHDRAWN &mdash; yesterday this row said the ES map carried no inferred stop of any kind above 7,782.11. That is no longer true. WITHDRAWN &mdash; <b>{{gone ES 7820.25}}</b> is no longer a cluster of any kind: the two cash-session turns printed on the exact top of the measurement have left the map, and the all-hours 70% band no longer reaches up to it. Two turns was thin evidence and a two-turn cluster is the easiest kind to run; the point it was making &mdash; that something was now there where nothing had been &mdash; is withdrawn, because that something has since left the map. What changed today is the distance to it, twice. The payrolls half-hour closed most of it, the afternoon closed more, and the cash session ended at 7,778.50 with spot at {{px ES}} in the evening stub &mdash; the top of the map is inside a normal session&rsquo;s reach for the first time since this branch was written. The structure between here and there turned over completely on the way and has since rebuilt: <b>{{pool ES 7764.68}}</b> was consumed at the print, rebuilt within the hour, consumed a SECOND time by the afternoon walk, and now carries {{turns ES 7764.68}} turns again &mdash; while the day&rsquo;s buying left two heavyweights bracketing the close, and the reopen has thickened the upper one. Well beneath, <b>{{gone ES 7755.66}}</b> no longer carries a pool at all &mdash; the structure that stood there has left the map, and it is NOT re-pointed at the nearest survivor: that one is four bins away and is the very level this paragraph has just been discussing, so moving the sentence onto it would make one level stand in for two. OVERTAKEN &mdash; the structure the Asian session built here was withdrawn when it left the map, and the reasoning for withdrawing it rather than re-anchoring was sound WHEN IT WAS WRITTEN: the only candidate then sat 1.25 bins away on a different turn count. It has since been overtaken by events. <b>{{pool ES 7784.43}}</b> is where that cluster keys now, on {{turns ES 7784.43}} turns. When this row called it a re-formation rather than a neighbour it stood just under half a bin from the price the withdrawal named; it has widened since, and the reading that justified the call is no longer the reading the map holds. Heavy enough is still true and is not the half spending itself. Close enough is, and when it runs out the page will stop being able to call the two one object &mdash; a boundary moving rather than a level going anywhere, which is the fact worth carrying &mdash; Friday&rsquo;s closing-auction cluster at the shelf top has been consumed on the way through. On <b>{{gone ES 7820.25}}</b>, nothing clusters any more: the turns printed at the top have left the map, and the claim is withdrawn rather than moved to a neighbour."],
      ["HSI", "Has the least to say about this branch and should not be asked. Nothing here resolves in HK hours."]
    ],
    invalid:"CALL: no chase, and on RTY no entry at all until branch A has happened first. RTY is beneath the <b>3,043.00</b> shelf it lost at the cash open, so a long here is not a breakout trade, it is a reclaim trade wearing a breakout&rsquo;s clothes, and the two want different stops. The sequence this branch NEEDED on RTY was a cash close back above 3,043.00, then the structure above that line taken and held through a full cash hour on real volume. THE SECOND LEG CANNOT BE SATISFIED AS WRITTEN: that pool is gone from the map, and it went without being traded through, so the condition has been voided by a structure change rather than resolved by the market. It is left as written rather than re-pointed at a nearer price &mdash; quietly moving a trigger onto a different object is how a condition stops meaning what it was agreed to mean. The restatement this row offered is no longer available: it would have pointed the second leg at <b>{{gone RTY 3046.45}}</b>, which is WITHDRAWN and holds nothing to point at. It is NOT re-pointed at {{pool RTY 3038.54}}, the object that consumed that band &mdash; moving a leg onto a structure that formed after the branch was written is the same quiet substitution this paragraph already refuses one sentence above. Only then is the stop under 3,043.00 &mdash; the same line as branch A, because a breakout that needs a different stop from the pullback it replaces is two trades pretending to be one. ES is the cleaner expression and always was on this branch, though it is now under its shelf rather than on it, which puts the trigger further away rather than making it different: a cash close above <b>7,787.50</b>, forty-eight points overhead from here. The stop moves DOWN rather than up, and the reason is a correction. Yesterday this row put it at 7,746.75 on the argument that the line had been closed through twice and reclaimed twice, so price argued about it rather than respecting it. Today it was closed through at 15:15Z and NOT reclaimed &mdash; price has left it, which makes it a poor stop and a worse story. A long that needs a reclaim of 7,787.50 to be right has no business being stopped out above the day&rsquo;s low, so the stop belongs under the session low at <b>7,724.25</b>: wider, fewer contracts, same money at risk. WHAT WOULD BREAK IT: ES back at its own high while RTY is still under 3,043.00. That is the non-confirmation, and note that it has swapped ends since yesterday &mdash; the worry used to be the small cap making a high the broad index could not follow, and today it is the broad index leaving the small cap under a level it already lost. Read it as a warning about the ES long rather than as a reason to buy RTY cheaply. Two measurement caveats belong on any branch that quotes an absence. Every unmapped claim above is a claim about COVERAGE, because the archive samples once every six minutes and can never prove a level was untouched. And that archive begins on 30 July with a thirteen-and-a-half-hour hole in it, plus a second unrecoverable hole of 9.83 hours across Tuesday&rsquo;s Asian and European sessions from the squash." }
];

/* Chain nodes that carry a defeat condition, registered by the chain painter
   and filled by drawChainBi once the ledger's evaluator is in scope. */
var CHAINBI = [];
var CHAINBI_LAST = [];
var CHAIN = [
  { node:"The long end", val:"10-yr 4.69% on the feed &middot; 30-yr 5.22% &mdash; and it IS on this feed now (treasury.gov daily), so the 5.15% regime line is testable from this page: it sits exactly on the 2004&ndash;2007 monthly plateau (5.15&ndash;5.20 at the tops of Aug 04, Jun 06, Jun 07). The last calendar month whose AVERAGE 30-yr sat at or above today&rsquo;s 5.22 was <b>July 2004</b> &mdash; twenty-two years &mdash; measured 7 Aug against the full monthly series since 1977, so &ldquo;multidecade&rdquo; is now a dated fact rather than a headline word &middot; Tuesday cash: RUT +1.850%, NDX beat it by 145bp",
    link:{ to:"RTY / small caps", st:"weakened", label:"TWICE NOW &mdash; a second day of rate relief and the Russell still did not lead",
      why:"The rule rewritten on 28 Jul after it cost money says small caps cannot lead while the long end is under pressure. It has now been handed two clean relief days in a row and it has survived both, and the way it survived them is the part worth being uncomfortable about. Monday: the curve fell five to six basis points at every tenor on the Hormuz de-escalation headline, 2s10s FLATTER rather than steeper, and RUT closed +1.72% against NDX +1.77% &mdash; a dead heat on the exact input this chain has said for a month is the only configuration that lets the Russell lead. Tuesday: another 5.9bp off the ten-year, 4.686% to 4.627%, and RUT closed +1.850%, which is a strong day in isolation and a loss of 145bp to the Nasdaq in the only comparison this node makes. Twice given what it asked for, twice no leadership. The stated break condition &mdash; the thirty-year closing below 5.15% &mdash; is not testable from this page today, because the thirty-year is not on this feed and the last reading held here is Monday&rsquo;s 5.23%. That figure is carried, not current, and it is not restated as though it were. Now the defect, which is in the node rather than in the market. The DEFEAT condition written on 3 Aug is RUT out-gaining NDX by more than 50bp on a day the long end is UNCHANGED &mdash; leadership without permission from rates. The ten-year has moved on every session since it was written. A condition that requires a flat tape in a week that has not produced one cannot fire, and a condition that cannot fire is indistinguishable from a condition that keeps passing, which is the failure this whole board is built against. It is left exactly as written. The alternative is to loosen &lsquo;unchanged&rsquo; until the week can satisfy it, and refitting a rail so a pinned component moves again is how a measurement turns into an opinion &mdash; the oil node below refuses that same trade and this one does not get an exemption for being inconvenient. What it needs is a flat-rates session, and if the week ends without one the condition should be re-specified deliberately and dated, not quietly widened. RE-SPECIFIED 13 AUG, and the paragraph above is left standing because it is the reason this was not done sooner. The week ended without a flat-rates session, which is the trigger that paragraph wrote for itself, so the condition is re-cut here rather than quietly widened &mdash; and the threshold is DERIVED rather than chosen. Across the ten completed US sessions the relay has banked (30 July to 12 August, which is a thin sample and is stated as one), the absolute day-over-day move in the long end sorts to 0, 0, 2, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 7, 8 basis points on the ten-year and 0, 1, 1, 3, 4, 5, 5, 6, 7 on the thirty. The bottom quartile of that pooled distribution is TWO BASIS POINTS at both the 25th and the 33rd percentile, so &ldquo;the long end did not move&rdquo; is now two basis points or less on BOTH tenors, measured on treasury.gov closes. Four of those ten sessions qualify, so the condition can fire, which the old one could not. THE NUMBER WAS TAKEN FROM THE DISTRIBUTION AND NOT FROM THE ANSWER, and the reader should be told that the answer is sensitive to it: at one basis point only two sessions qualify and Tuesday 11 August is not one of them; at two, it is. One basis point is roughly the tenth percentile, not the bottom quartile, so it would have to be argued for rather than derived. WHAT THE RE-SPECIFIED CONDITION SAYS ABOUT TUESDAY. On 11 August the ten-year moved two basis points and the thirty-year one, both inside the threshold, and RUT beat NDX by 66 basis points. The DEFEAT fires. The rule that small caps cannot lead while the long end is under pressure has been beaten once, on a session where rates gave no permission either way. THE READING THAT DISAGREES, stated rather than buried, because two honest readings of one sentence is exactly the case this board says to disclose. &ldquo;Unchanged&rdquo; can be read as a magnitude &mdash; the long end did not move &mdash; or as a direction: the long end did not EASE, since the rule has always been about small caps needing relief. Under the direction reading Tuesday is excluded, because both tenors fell, and the node survives. The magnitude reading is adopted, for a reason that is about the rule rather than about the outcome: the node&rsquo;s own examples of &ldquo;clean relief days&rdquo; were five and six basis point moves, so a two basis point drift is inside the noise the rule was never about &mdash; and the direction reading admits one session in ten, which puts the condition straight back where it started, unable to fire. ONE DEFEAT IS NOT A RETIREMENT. It is one session, recorded with its terms, on a rule that has been handed relief twice and refused to lead both times. What it removes is the position this node was in for a week: a claim nothing could contradict." },
    bi:[ { n:"the long-end DEFEAT &mdash; RUT beating NDX by more than 50bp on a session the long end moved 2bp or less (re-specified 13 Aug)",
           legs:[ { src:"chgspread", sym:"RUT", vs:"NDX", op:"above", v:50,
                    label:"RUT over NDX", dp:1, suf:"bp" },
                  { src:"curvechg", sym:"Y10", op:"not_above", v:2,
                    label:"the 10-year&rsquo;s move", dp:1, suf:"bp" },
                  { src:"curvechg", sym:"Y30", op:"not_above", v:2,
                    label:"the 30-year&rsquo;s move", dp:1, suf:"bp" } ] } ] },
  { node:"Megacap concentration", val:"Tuesday cash: NDX beat SPX by 152bp &middot; RUT +1.850% &mdash; the dispersion came back and the re-arm still did not fire",
    link:{ to:"Index level vs breadth", st:"broken", label:"STILL RETIRED &mdash; one leg of the re-arm arrived, the other did not, which is why it was written with two",
      why:"This node was retired on 3 Aug because Friday did not fit it, and the re-arm condition was written down at the same moment: an up day in which NDX out-gains SPX by more than 50bp WHILE RUT is negative. Tuesday delivered the first leg emphatically and the second not at all. NDX beat SPX by 152bp, three times the threshold and the widest dispersion since Friday. RUT closed +1.850%, one of its better sessions of the week. So the tape produced concentration in the growth complex without producing the breadth failure that would make concentration the story, and a node re-armed on the first leg alone would now be back on the board describing a day the small-cap index rose 1.85%. That is the entire argument for writing re-arm conditions with two legs and for not editing one when the other arrives. Dispersion is not direction: everything went up, and the Nasdaq went up more. The live single-name evidence cuts the same way and is worth more than the dispersion figure. AMD reported Tuesday after the close and beat and raised &mdash; revenue $11.54B against roughly $11.25B to $11.31B expected, adjusted EPS $1.66 against $1.60 to $1.62, data-centre revenue doubled, and Q3 guided to about $13.0B plus or minus $300M against a $12.51B consensus &mdash; and fell six and a half to seven per cent in the after-hours session, having come into the print up 142.2% on the year. Palantir reported Monday, beat on every line, guided Q3 to $2.16B, and rose about 10% toward $140. A beat-and-raise sold and a beat-and-raise bought in the same forty-eight hours is a statement about positioning rather than about fundamentals, and the one that was sold was the one that had already tripled. This board should be explicit that it holds no view on AMD. What it holds is the mechanism: if the growth complex is priced past its own delivery, the NDX-over-RUT ordering can inflect without the long end doing anything at all &mdash; which is precisely the test the node above cannot get a flat-rates day to run. Re-arm is unchanged: NDX over SPX by more than 50bp on an up day with RUT negative." } },
  { node:"Dollar and gold", val:"gold 4,229.40, +3.27% from Tuesday&rsquo;s 4,095.40 close, session LOW 4,121.60 &mdash; the whole range above the 4,119.40 trigger &middot; DXY 99.803, session high 99.90, never saw 100",
    link:{ to:"Risk appetite", st:"transmitting", label:"CONFIRMED &mdash; the condition was written in advance and both legs cleared it by a margin",
      why:"CONFIRMED, on a condition written in advance and left alone. This node fired on Thursday because the dollar fell while yields rose and gold was bid, which is an inflation risk premium being demanded rather than financial conditions easing. It lapsed on Friday when the dollar took it back above 100, and the lapse was published, because a warning that never stands down is not a warning, it is a mood. It re-armed on Monday with both legs inside a rounding error &mdash; DXY under 100 by four hundredths, the thirty-year over 5.20% by three basis points &mdash; and this board said out loud that a condition met by 0.04 and 0.03 is met and is also one ordinary hour from not being met. What was missing was the third leg, and the confirm test was stated as exactly that: gold through 4,119.40, the overnight high, while the dollar stays beneath 100. Both halves are now true and the margin is not a rounding error. Gold is 4,229.40, up 3.27% from Tuesday&rsquo;s 4,095.40 close, and the relevant number is not the last print but the session LOW of 4,121.60 &mdash; the entire overnight range traded above the trigger, so this is not a level touched once on a wick. The dollar never saw 100: DXY is 99.803 against a session high of 99.90. Two things have to be said against it, and they are said here rather than after the fact. First, this board cannot see the third leg it was worrying about on Monday: the thirty-year is not on this feed, so whether yields are still elevated while the dollar falls &mdash; the actual shape that made this node mean something &mdash; is not readable today, and the ten-year falling 5.9bp is the wrong sign for the story. A confirm on two legs of a three-leg thesis is a confirm of what was written, and it is weaker than what was meant. Second, this board has not established WHY gold moved and should not furnish a reason it did not measure; a search returned rate-expectation and dollar-weakness framing and nothing that settles it. What can be stated is the mechanical read-across: a three per cent day in the metal with the dollar pinned under 100 is the market paying up for a hedge on the night before ADP and two days before payrolls, and it is not a risk-on tape wearing a different hat. What breaks it now: the dollar closing back above 100, or gold giving back the 4,119.40 line on a session close &mdash; a hundred and ten dollars of room, which is the difference between this reading and Monday&rsquo;s." } },
  { node:"Oil", val:"broken since Sunday&rsquo;s gap under 81 &middot; Tuesday round-tripped 82.25 to 74.25 &middot; re-arm rail 85.94 is eleven and a half dollars away",
    link:{ to:"Headline inflation floor", st:"broken", label:"BROKEN, AND FURTHER FROM RE-ARMING THAN AT ANY POINT SINCE IT WAS WRITTEN",
      why:"This node was written against a floor near 83 and it named its own invalidation in advance: a session close under 81 takes the war premium out of the inflation argument entirely. That break came on Sunday&rsquo;s reopen as a gap &mdash; 84.67 at 22:06Z, 79.55 six minutes later, through 81.51 and 81 and 80 in a single print &mdash; and the node has been broken since. The scored MISS on the Friday call stays exactly as it was scored: a call is judged at its resolution and never re-judged when the market comes round to it over a weekend, and pretending otherwise is how a scorecard becomes fiction. What Tuesday added is a full round trip. Crude ran back to 82.25 and then gave all of it up, trading down to 74.25 &mdash; eight dollars in a session, on the Iran and Oman interim-deal headlines that Trump was trailed to speak to at 20:30Z that evening. The consequence for the chain is unchanged and now larger: with crude beneath the pre-strike floor there is no commodity floor under headline inflation this week, which makes today&rsquo;s ISM services prices-paid component a cleaner read of underlying pressure than it would otherwise be. One thing this node has to disclose about its own evidence. The run to 82.25 falls inside the archive&rsquo;s only unrecoverable hole &mdash; 9.83 hours from 00:25Z to 10:15Z on 4 Aug, destroyed when the droplet squashed the repository and took 4,001 commits with it. So the collapse out of that high is reconstructable from the record and the spike into it is not: the 82.25 figure is carried from the session ledger rather than from anything this page can re-derive. Re-arms above 85.94, a rail dated 30 Jul, and crude is eleven and a half dollars beneath it &mdash; which is the furthest this node has been from re-arming since it was written, and the rail is left uncut on purpose." } },
  { node:"Everything", val:"a payrolls week in which rates have eased twice and small caps have led neither time, no commodity floor under headline inflation, one node confirming on a condition it wrote in advance, and a scheduled headline into the thinnest book of the day", link:null }
];

var CALLS = [
  { d:"7 Aug", cat:"market", c:"Payrolls continuation &mdash; short RTY on a 30-minute close below 3,004.90 after the 12:30Z print, first qualifying close the bar ending 13:00Z; no ES trade between 7,724.25 and 7,745.00; no MHI trade", r:"notrade", o:"NO TRADE, and the entry condition is the whole reason. Targets were 2,992.00, 2,988.00 and a 2,951.11 runner, stop a 30-minute close back above 3,012.00, invalidation a full 30-minute close above 3,015.00. The judged leg was written as a precondition rather than a hope: the trade was live only if the survey internals printed soft. They did not. Payrolls came in hot enough to bid four contracts into their 84th-to-95th percentiles over the 42 minutes bracketing the print, and RTY went the other way from the first bar. THE TRIGGER NEVER FIRED, and it was not close. The first qualifying 30-minute close, the bar ending 13:00Z, printed <b>3,034.50</b> &mdash; not merely above the 3,004.90 entry but 19.5 points above the 3,015.00 invalidation, so the call was dead at the first moment it could be read. The lowest RTY traded at any point after the window opened was <b>3,023.00</b>, eighteen points above the entry, so no entry style would have filled: not a close, not a touch, not a limit sitting at the level. THE PART WORTH KEEPING is what a careless reading would have said. RTY DID trade 3,004.90 &mdash; it printed 3,002.00 at 00:20Z, twelve hours before the trade window opened, on the overnight session this call explicitly stood aside from. A status chip derived from the trailing-day range, which is what this page had until 8 Aug, would have reported the trigger level TRADED and left the reader to work out that it happened half a day early. The window-aware chip shipped that weekend reads the 30-minute closes inside the entry&rsquo;s own stated hours and reported exactly what the archive reports here: invalidated at 13:00Z, never triggered. This resolution is the first live test of that machinery and it agrees with the tape. HOW TO SCORE THIS, and it is a new column rather than a judgement call. The directional read was WRONG &mdash; the tape went up hard and a looser entry would have paid for it. The entry discipline was RIGHT and cost nothing. Filing that as a hit would inflate the record with a trade nobody took; filing it as a miss would punish the one habit worth building. It is counted in neither, and the hit rate above is computed without it. The other two legs stand as written: ES was left alone between 7,724.25 and 7,745.00 and the 13:00Z close at 7,770.25 took it out of that band, which retired the no-trade by its own terms rather than converting it into an entry; MHI was left alone because Hong Kong was shut for the print, which is a decision the clock makes rather than the analyst." },
  { d:"6 Aug", cat:"market", c:"Short RTY on acceptance beneath 3,023.00 &mdash; the shelf broke, the hollow underneath it did NOT fill, and the delta profile says the whole board is standing on a shelf built on top of a void rather than on a floor", r:"miss", o:"Written FLAT, as a re-entry rather than a management decision. The evidence is a bin-for-bin difference of the two most recent profiles, which needs no value-area anchor and is not open to interpretation: every contract traded between the two builds &mdash; 535,732 ES and 52,015 RTY &mdash; landed inside twelve ES bins and eighteen RTY bins, and NOT ONE BIN DECLINED ANYWHERE. That is a shelf thickening on top of a hollow, not a hollow filling. Beneath the session floor RTY falls thirty-one bins from 3,022.00 to the first bin holding even one per cent at 2,991.00, with 8.53% of the window in the gap; ES falls forty-four bins from 7,742.50 to 7,632.50 with 12.23% in the gap. The trade is the retest of 3,024.60&ndash;3,027.30 once 3,023.00 is lost, or a sell-stop at 3,022.50 for anyone who will not sit for the retest. T1 2,999.00, T2 2,991.00, runner 2,936.00. The stop is a 30-minute close above 3,031.20 and that is deliberately not a touch. NO ES TRADE: spot sits nine points from the six-day point of control, which is the definition of no edge. NO HSI TRADE. The reaction function is the other half of the case &mdash; ADP printed 44,000 against a 68,000 forecast and ES moved 2.50 points over forty-two minutes, a 35th-percentile move; ISM services then missed by four tenths with the employment sub at 47.4 and ES fell 10.00, an 80th-percentile move. The tape ignored the jobs miss and sold the services miss, which says it is repricing the SLOPE rather than the number, and AMD beating every line and falling 8.94% after hours says the same thing from the other side of the market. RESOLVED 6 Aug, a MISS, and the way it missed is worth more than the fact that it did. The entry filled: the 30-minute bar to 13:30Z closed 3,022.60, beneath 3,023.00, and the retest zone 3,024.60&ndash;3,027.30 was traded inside the next half hour. The stop then filled too. A 30-minute close above 3,031.20 was written as the stop deliberately, to avoid being taken out on a touch, and it printed TWICE &mdash; 3,036.80 to 14:30Z and 3,031.70 to 15:00Z. Out at roughly eleven to fourteen points against. Direction was right and the trade still lost, which is the only outcome that teaches anything: price never traded 3,043.00, turned at 3,041.00, and has since made the day&rsquo;s low at 3,015.00 &mdash; below the entry, on its way toward a T1 of 2,999.00 that is still eighteen points away. The defect is the stop&rsquo;s LOCATION rather than its timeframe. 3,031.20 sits inside the hollow, three quarters of a point above where 3,030.44 sat before the payrolls bid emptied it &mdash; no turns are listed there now &mdash; with the 3,038.31 pool and its nine turns above that. A stop placed just over a stop pool, inside the one stretch of the map where price has no reason to slow down, is a stop placed where the sweep goes. The rule this earns: in a low-volume pocket the stop belongs ABOVE the pools that pocket contains, not between them &mdash; here that meant 3,043.00, the shelf floor, which was never touched. Wider stop, same size, fewer contracts. That is a real cost and it is smaller than this one was.",
    bi:[ { n:"price trades through 3,031.20 &mdash; the early reading, stricter than the stop",
           win:[13.5, 20], from:1785974400,
           legs:[ { sym:"RTY", src:"px", unit:"quoted", op:"above", v:3031.2,
                    label:"RTY", dp:2, suf:"" } ] },
         { n:"the 30-minute close above 3,031.20 that is the actual stop",
           why:"the ledger measures five-minute bar extremes and has no notion of a thirty-minute close, so it can only read the TOUCH above. Those are different instruments and this board has already paid for confusing them once: on 31 July a call defined on half-hour closes survived while a stop resting at the same number was taken out an hour into the session and did not participate in the thirty-four-point recovery. The condition above will fire before this one does, every time, and it is not a substitute for it." }
 ] },
  { d:"6 Aug", cat:"system", c:"The quote archive stamps thirteen prices with one timestamp &mdash; the instant of the fetch &mdash; so every US futures price in it has been filed ten minutes late since the archive began, and the board published a New York session low that was made in London", r:"hit",
    o:"Each row of archive/quotes is one fetch: a timestamp, then thirteen prices hung off it. The file those prices are lifted FROM, latest/free.json, carries a per-symbol ts and age_s and a meta note in plain English &mdash; &lsquo;US index futures are exchange-delayed ~15min. Overnight ranges and levels are unaffected by the delay; the last tick is.&rsquo; The live page honours that, because the live page still has age_s. The archiver throws it away and stamps everything with the moment the request returned, and every consumer downstream inherits a clock belonging to the observer rather than to the market. THE DELAY IS MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. ES and RTY are the only symbols with both a quote series and a bar series, so they are the only place the offset can be seen: ask what share of quotes fall inside the high-low range of the bar N minutes earlier, and at zero it is 44&ndash;49%, which is what noise looks like, while at ten minutes it is 99.7% of 932 on ES and 95.9% on RTY, on every day of the archive. That is an identification, not a fit. NQ and YM inherit it, and the inheritance is CHECKED rather than assumed: all four futures come back under one ts and one src in latest/free.json, and the day that splits, the build fails. WHAT IT COST. TYPQ &mdash; the displacement threshold that decides continuation from reversal &mdash; was understated 58.2% on NQ and 84.2% on YM, and was exactly 0.00 for HSI, NDX, RUT, SPX and VIX, because a repeated quote contributes a zero-width step and those five repeat 63&ndash;86% of the time. A threshold of zero resolves every sweep as a continuation on the first sample that moves. And the print that set NQ&rsquo;s 4 August NEW YORK low was stamp